hed; RV 1.185.6 is now support only. The genitive stands in the Latin alone (diuum genitor). (2) The agent noun is the suffix-accented *ǵenh₁tḗr (pie-lex-gjenh1texr), as Ved. janitā́ (oxytone) in the cited stanza; the root-accented ǵénh₁tōr is contradicted by it. Lat. genitor does not show the accent. Caveats: Iuppiter carries *dyēws and *ph₂tḗr only as compound members; Catullus 64 is a Hellenistic-modelled epyllion, and diuum genitor may belong to the divom pater type that Ennius modelled on Homer. Builder (checked after the revision): pie-lex-gjenh1texr gets level root only, as gjenh1toxr did, and *ph₂tḗr gets none (Iuppiter carries it only as a compound member).", "review": "weak", "eval_grade": false}
